# Incident Runbook: {{ .Service.Name }}

## Ownership

- Team: {{ .Service.Team }}
- Owner: {{ .Service.Owner }}
- Pager: {{ if .Service.Pager }}{{ .Service.Pager }}{{ else }}TODO: register a pager rotation{{ end }}
- Namespace: {{ .Service.Namespace }}
- Environment: {{ .Service.Environment }}

## SLO

- Availability target: {{ printf "%.2f" .Service.SLOTarget }}% over 30 days
- p95 latency target: {{ .Service.SLOLatencyP95Ms }}ms
- Error-rate threshold: {{ .Service.SLOErrorRate }}%
- Deployment strategy: {{ .Service.DeploymentStrategy }}
- Rollback on failure: {{ .Service.RollbackOnFailure }}

## Triage

1. Check the current rollout: `kubectl -n {{ .Service.Namespace }} rollout status deploy/{{ .Service.Name }}`
2. Check the RolloutGuard: `kubectl -n {{ .Service.Namespace }} describe rolloutguard {{ .Service.Name }}-rollout`
3. Inspect recent events: `kubectl -n {{ .Service.Namespace }} get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp`
4. Check pod health: `kubectl -n {{ .Service.Namespace }} get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/name={{ .Service.Name }}`
5. Check measured SLO state: `attesta slo status {{ .Service.Name }}`

## Rollback

Roll back in the cluster:

```bash
kubectl -n {{ .Service.Namespace }} rollout undo deploy/{{ .Service.Name }}
```

Record the decision in Attesta (this records a request; it does not change the cluster):

```bash
curl -X POST -H "X-Attesta-Token: $ATTESTA_API_TOKEN" "$ATTESTA_API_URL/api/v1/services/{{ .Service.Name }}/rollback"
```

## Escalation

Page {{ .Service.Team }} if the SLO burn continues for more than 10 minutes or the rollback fails.
